# Preparation of Electrocompetent *E. coli* BL21

---

## Day 1 – Overnight Culture and Reagents

### Equipment

- 37°C incubator (for plates)
- 37°C shaking incubator (for liquid culture)
- Sterile inoculating loops or pipette tips
- 15 mL sterile culture tubes
- 50 mL sterile Falcon tubes
- Duran bottles 500 mL, 200 mL
- 500 mL culture flasks

### Reagents

- *E. coli* BL21 (DE3) stock (glycerol stock or frozen stock)
- LB agar plates (no antibiotics)
- LB agar + Kanamycin plates
- LB agar + Carbenicillin plates (or other commonly used antibiotic plates)
- LB broth (no antibiotics)
- Distilled H₂O
- Glycerol (100%)

### Procedure

(1) Streak plates to check stock quality  
Using a sterile loop or tip, swab *E. coli* BL21 from the frozen glycerol stock. Be sure not to contaminate the stock. Inoculate 5 mL LB broth (no antibiotics) in a sterile culture tube. Using a sterile loop or tip, swab *E. coli* BL21 from the frozen glycerol stock. Streak onto three plates: LB agar (no antibiotics), LB agar + Kanamycin, and LB agar + Carbenicillin (or equivalent antibiotics used in the lab). The purpose of the plating is to check for contamination.

(2) Incubate plates and overnight culture  
Place plates inverted in a 37°C incubator. Incubate overnight 5 mL (approximately 16 hours) in a 37°C shaking incubator.

(3) Prepare for Day 2 – Solutions and materials  
Prepare 400 mL LB medium in a 500 mL bottle. Prepare 10% glycerol solution by mixing 10 mL glycerol with 90 mL distilled H₂O. Sterilize these (autoclave), label, and store at 4°C. Ensure you have at least 150 sterile 1.5 mL microcentrifuge tubes ready (factory-sterile or autoclaved). Autoclave 500 mL Erlenmeyer flask (or equivalent for 200 mL culture), seal with aluminum foil. Place four new, sealed, sterile 50 mL Falcon tubes in the 4°C fridge to chill (four tubes per 200 mL of final culture). Pre-chill aluminum beads 4°C, and –20°C gel packs/blocks to make sure you will have plenty of “ice” available for Day 2.

---

## Day 2 – Preparation of Electrocompetent Cells

### Equipment

- 500 mL Erlenmeyer flask (or equivalent for 200 mL culture)
- 37°C shaking incubator
- Spectrophotometer and cuvettes (for OD₆₀₀ measurements)
- Chilled 50 mL sterile Falcon tubes (four per 200 mL of culture)
- Refrigerated centrifuge with rotor for 50 mL tubes
- Chilled sterile 1.5 mL microcentrifuge tubes

### Reagents

- Overnight *E. coli* BL21 (DE3) culture from Day 1
- LB broth (no antibiotics)
- Chilled, sterile 10% glycerol (4°C)
- Known plasmid for competency test (e.g., GFP plasmid)
- LB agar plates (no antibiotics)
- LB agar plates with appropriate antibiotics (e.g., Kanamycin, Carbenicillin)

### Procedure

(1) Grow culture to mid-log phase (OD₆₀₀ ≈ 0.4–0.9)  
Turn on the centrifuge and set temperature to 2°C. This takes about 1 hour to reach. Add 200 mL LB broth (no antibiotics) to a 500 mL flask. Inoculate with 2 mL of the overnight culture from Day 1. Ensure sterility and seal. Place the flask in a 37°C shaking incubator (200–250 rpm). Measure OD₆₀₀ periodically using a spectrophotometer. Allow the culture to grow until OD₆₀₀ ≈ 0.35. This typically takes about 2–3 hours. Once OD₆₀₀ is above 0.2, check every 15–20 minutes and avoid exceeding OD₆₀₀ = 0.9. Doubling time is approximately 20–30 minutes.

(2) Chill the culture  
When OD₆₀₀ ≈ 0.35 is reached, place the entire flask in a 4°C refrigerator in a bucket of chilled “ice”. Chill for at least 10 minutes, ideally about 30 minutes, gently swirling occasionally to cool evenly. The target is to cool the culture below ~8°C to stop growth.

(3) Harvest cells  
Label four pre-chilled 50 mL sterile Falcon tubes. Distribute the chilled culture into these tubes (approximately 45 mL per tube). From this step onward, keep all tubes, reagents, and cells on ice whenever they are not in the chilled centrifuge. Centrifuge at 3500 rpm for 10 minutes at 2°C. Carefully pour off the supernatant without disturbing the cell pellet. If the pellet is loose, leave a small volume and remove the supernatant carefully with a pipette.

(4) 10% glycerol wash (3×)  
Add 20 mL of ice-cold 10% glycerol to each pellet. Gently resuspend the cells in each tube by pipetting up and down or slowly swirling. Avoid vigorous vortexing. Place the tubes on “ice” (and in the 4°C fridge) and incubate for 30 minutes, keeping them colder than 8°C as much as possible (but above 0°C). Centrifuge again at 3500 rpm for 10 minutes at 2°C. Carefully remove and discard the supernatant. The pellet will be much looser now. Repeat this step 3 times to remove any salts from the solution. These would cause the sample to arc when electroporated. Once repeated 3 times, add 500 µL of 10% glycerol to each tube (100 µL per 10 mL of original culture).

(5) Aliquot competent cells  
Optionally, pool the resuspended cells from all Falcon tubes into one pre-chilled sterile tube to make aliquoting easier, and keep this tube on ice. Label storage box for pre-chilled 1.5 mL microcentrifuge tubes in advance with strain, date, and your initials to save time. Dispense 50 μL of the resuspended cells into each 1.5 mL tube while keeping both the master suspension and the tubes on ice. Expect this step to take up to about 1 hour; prepare extra ice so the cells remain cold the entire time.

(6) Freeze competent aliquots  
Confirm all remaining aliquots are stored with labels with strain, date, and your initials. Keep tubes on ice while organizing them into a storage box or rack. If possible, snap-freeze the tubes on dry ice or in a dry ice/ethanol bath before moving them to the –80°C freezer. If snap-freezing is not available, place them directly at –80°C while minimizing time off ice. Store long-term at –80°C and avoid repeated freeze–thaw cycles.

(7) Test competency  
(After freezing) Use 3 aliquot tubes for a test transformation with a known plasmid (e.g., GFP plasmid). Perform a standard electroporation protocol for competent *E. coli* (follow your lab’s specific timing and temperature steps). Plate transformed cells on:  
- LB (no antibiotics) to assess general viability and background growth.  
- LB + Kanamycin.  
- LB + Carbenicillin (or other appropriate antibiotic(s) for the plasmid used).  

Ensure plates are at room temperature before plating.





